      "pin": false,
      "content": "\r\n## The One Word That Unlocks the Whole Board\r\n\r\nEvery NYT Strands puzzle hides one special answer called the **Spangram**. Unlike the theme words, the Spangram stretches all the way across the 6×8 grid, touching two opposite sides — top-to-bottom or left-to-right. It's highlighted in gold rather than blue, and it names or sums up the puzzle's theme directly.\r\n\r\nIf theme words are the locks, the Spangram is the master key. Find it early and the rest of the puzzle stops feeling like a guessing game.\r\n\r\n## Why Finding It First Changes Everything\r\n\r\nThree concrete advantages come from locating the Spangram before anything else:\r\n\r\n1. **It confirms the theme.** The theme clue is often a pun or loose hint. The Spangram is the literal answer to \"what is this puzzle about?\" Once you read it, every other word you're hunting for snaps into a clearer category.\r\n2. **It splits the board.** Because the Spangram runs edge to edge, it physically divides the grid into regions. Theme words live around it, and that partition shrinks the area you have to scan for each remaining word.\r\n3. **It uses a big chunk of letters.** Spangrams are usually 7–12 letters long. Placing it removes a large set of letters from play, and since every letter on the board belongs to exactly one answer, what's left is far more constrained.\r\n\r\n## How to Actually Find It\r\n\r\nThe Spangram has a unique fingerprint: it's the only answer guaranteed to touch two opposite edges. Use that.\r\n\r\n- **Start at the edges, not the middle.** Pick a side and look for a long word that could begin there and travel to the far side. Corner and edge letters connect to fewer neighbors, so there are fewer paths to check.\r\n- **Think in the theme's vocabulary.** If the clue hints at \"things in a kitchen,\" brainstorm 8–12 letter kitchen phrases and see whether any could span the board.\r\n- **Expect it to twist.** Like all Strands answers, the Spangram rarely runs in a straight line. It can zigzag, turn diagonally, and double back — as long as it ends up touching the opposite edge.\r\n- **Watch for two-word Spangrams.** Many Spangrams are compound phrases (e.g. \"FULLMOON,\" \"ICECREAM\"). If no single long word fits, try a pair of shorter words read as one.\r\n\r\n## Reading the Direction\r\n\r\nOnce you spot a candidate, check how it's oriented. A Spangram that runs mostly **vertically** starts from the top or bottom edge; one that runs mostly **horizontally** starts from the left or right. Knowing the orientation tells you which edge to begin tracing from and roughly how many rows or columns it has to cross — a useful sanity check before you commit to a path.\r\n\r\nThis is exactly the kind of detail our daily hint pages surface for you: each puzzle's Spangram direction and starting edge are spelled out so you can verify your trace.\r\n\r\n## A Common Mistake: Chasing Theme Words First\r\n\r\nBeginners often find two or three short theme words, feel good, and then stall. The problem is that without the Spangram, you don't have a firm grip on the theme, so you waste time second-guessing whether a word you found is actually part of the puzzle. Flip the order. Spend your first couple of minutes specifically hunting the long edge-to-edge word. Even if you don't nail it immediately, the search forces you to engage with the theme at the right level of abstraction.\r\n\r\n## When You're Truly Stuck\r\n\r\nIf the Spangram won't reveal itself, earn a hint the designed way: find any valid non-theme word of four or more letters. Every few of those highlights a letter in an answer, and a single revealed letter along an edge often exposes where the Spangram begins. There's no shame in it — the hint system is part of the game, not a failure state.\r\n\r\n## Put It Into Practice\r\n\r\nThe Spangram-first habit is the single biggest upgrade most solvers can make. Try it on [today's Strands hints](/strands-hint-today), where you can reveal the Spangram's direction and length before seeing the word itself, or study how past Spangrams were built in the [Strands archive](/strands-hint). After a week of leading with the Spangram, you'll wonder how you ever solved these puzzles any other way.\r\n",
